# Git Branching and Merging Hands-on Lab

Objective:
To learn how to create a branch, switch between branches, make changes in a branch, merge the branch into the main branch, view commit history, and delete the merged branch.

Prerequisites:
Git installed
GitHub account
Git Bash
Local Git repository

Procedure

Step 1: Checked the current branch.
Command:
git branch

Step 2: Created a new branch named GitNewBranch.
Command:
git branch GitNewBranch

Step 3: Listed all local branches and verified the current branch.
Command:
git branch

Step 4: Switched to the new branch.
Command:
git switch GitNewBranch

Step 5: Created a new file in the branch.
Command:
echo "This file was created in GitNewBranch" > branchfile.txt

Step 6: Added the file to the staging area.
Command:
git add branchfile.txt

Step 7: Committed the changes.
Command:
git commit -m "Added branch file"

Step 8: Checked the repository status.
Command:
git status

Step 9: Switched back to the main branch.
Command:
git switch main

Step 10: Listed all available branches.
Command:
git branch

Step 11: Compared the differences between the main branch and GitNewBranch.
Command:
git diff main..GitNewBranch

Step 12: Visual comparison using P4Merge was not performed because P4Merge was not installed.

Step 13: Merged GitNewBranch into the main branch.
Command:
git merge GitNewBranch

Step 14: Viewed the commit history in graphical format.
Command:
git log --oneline --graph --decorate

Step 15: Deleted the merged branch.
Command:
git branch -d GitNewBranch

Step 16: Checked the repository status.
Command:
git status

Step 17: Pushed the latest changes to the GitHub repository.
Command:
git push

Result:
Successfully created a new branch, added and committed changes, switched branches, compared differences, merged the branch into the main branch, viewed the commit history, deleted the merged branch, and pushed the updated repository to GitHub successfully.


